   "id": "h2-9",
   "name": "Water in food and in the body"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Water makes up more than half of the body's weight.\nSources: drinking water and other drinks; foods with a lot of water: oranges, watermelon, pawpaw, pineapple, tomatoes, cucumber, leafy vegetables, soups and milk. The body also makes a little water when it uses food.\nUses of water in food preparation: cooking (boiling, steaming), washing food, making drinks, dissolving salt and sugar, softening dry foods (soaking beans, garri).\nFunctions of water in the body:\n• a part of every cell, blood and body fluids;\n• transports nutrients and oxygen in the blood;\n• removes wastes (urine, sweat);\n• controls body temperature (sweating);\n• helps digestion and prevents constipation; lubricates the joints.\nWe lose water in urine, sweat and breath, so we should drink clean water regularly, more in hot weather and after exercise. Too little water causes dehydration (thirst, headache, dark urine).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "f": "an orange"
  },
  "problem": "Give two ways in which eating {f} helps the body.",
  "steps": [
   "An orange is mostly water, so it helps to replace the water we lose in sweat and urine.",
   "It also gives vitamin C."
  ],
  "answer": "It supplies water (and vitamin C).",
  "checks": []
 },
